  "title": "Composition: Order Matters",
  "courseId": "function-analysis",
  "chapterId": "ch4-composition-depth",
  "minutes": 9,
  "steps": [
    {
      "id": "c1",
      "kind": "concept",
      "body": "**f(g(x))** means g runs **first**, and its output becomes f's input. Order is everything: f(g(x)) and g(f(x)) are usually **different functions**. Read compositions inside-out, like nested parentheses.",
      "figure": "fna-compose-order"
    },
    {
      "id": "i1",
      "kind": "interactive",
      "body": "Inside first.",
      "widget": {
        "type": "derivativeTrace",
        "fn": "square",
        "mode": "point",
        "targetX": 3,
        "start": 0,
        "prompt": "f(g(3)) works from the inside out. Trace g(x) = x² to x = 3 and read the height — that height becomes f's input next.",
        "successFeedback": "g(3) = 9. Now feed that INTO f: f(9) = 2(9) − 1 = 17. Composition order matters because the output of the inner function becomes the input of the outer one — g first, then f on whatever g handed back.",
        "lowFeedback": "Still short of x = 3.",
        "highFeedback": "Past x = 3 now."
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "k1",
